What is Helm in Kubernetes?

Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes. It simplifies deploying, managing, and upgrading Kubernetes applications by packaging all the required Kubernetes YAML files into a single reusable package called a Chart.

Think of it like:

APT for Ubuntu
YUM/DNF for RHEL
npm for Node.js
Helm for Kubernetes

Instead of manually applying multiple YAML files, you can install an application with a single Helm command.

Why use Helm?

Without Helm, deploying an application might require:

Deployment YAML
Service YAML
ConfigMap YAML
Secret YAML
Ingress YAML
Persistent Volume YAML

You would apply each file individually:

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f service.yaml
kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml
...

With Helm, everything is packaged into a chart:

helm install myapp ./mychart
Helm Architecture
             Helm CLI
                 |
                 |
        Kubernetes API Server
                 |
           Kubernetes Cluster

Helm 3 does not require a server-side component (Helm 2 used Tiller, which has been removed).

Helm Chart

A Chart is a collection of Kubernetes manifests organized into a directory structure.

Example:

mychart/
│
├── Chart.yaml
├── values.yaml
├── templates/
│   ├── deployment.yaml
│   ├── service.yaml
│   ├── ingress.yaml
│   └── configmap.yaml
└── charts/
Important Files
1. Chart.yaml

Contains metadata.

Example:

apiVersion: v2
name: myapp
version: 1.0.0
description: Sample Helm Chart
2. values.yaml

Stores configurable values.

Example:

replicaCount: 2

image:
  repository: nginx
  tag: latest

service:
  type: ClusterIP
  port: 80
3. templates/

Contains Kubernetes YAML templates.

Example:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

spec:
  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}

{{ }} is Helm's template syntax.

Helm Workflow
values.yaml
      |
      ↓
Templates + Values
      |
      ↓
Rendered Kubernetes YAML
      |
      ↓
kubectl applies to cluster
Common Helm Commands
Add a Repository
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
Update Repositories
helm repo update
Search Charts
helm search repo nginx
Install a Chart
helm install my-nginx bitnami/nginx
List Installed Releases
helm list
Upgrade a Release
helm upgrade my-nginx bitnami/nginx
Uninstall a Release
helm uninstall my-nginx
Roll Back to a Previous Version
helm rollback my-nginx 1
Helm Release

A Release is a running instance of a Helm chart.

Example:

Chart: nginx

Release 1 → dev-nginx

Release 2 → test-nginx

Release 3 → prod-nginx

The same chart can be installed multiple times with different release names and configurations.

Overriding Values

You can override default values during installation.

Example:

helm install my-nginx bitnami/nginx \
  --set replicaCount=3

Or use a custom values file:

helm install my-nginx bitnami/nginx \
-f production-values.yaml
Creating Your Own Chart

Create a new chart:

helm create myapp

This generates a starter chart with the standard directory structure.

Helm Template

Render templates locally without installing:

helm template myapp .

This helps verify the generated Kubernetes manifests before deployment.

Helm Lint

Check your chart for errors:

helm lint .
Advantages of Helm
Simplifies Kubernetes application deployment.
Supports reusable, versioned application packages (Charts).
Enables environment-specific configurations using values.yaml.
Supports easy upgrades and rollbacks.
Reduces duplication through templates.
Widely used by the Kubernetes ecosystem.
Example: Installing NGINX
# Add repository
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami

# Update repository
helm repo update

# Install NGINX
helm install my-nginx bitnami/nginx

# Verify
kubectl get pods
kubectl get svc
Helm vs kubectl
Feature	kubectl	Helm
Deploy resources	Yes	Yes
Package applications	No	Yes
Templating	No	Yes
Version management	Limited	Yes
Rollback	Manual	Built-in
Reusable deployments	No	Yes
Configuration management	Manual	values.yaml
Summary

Helm is the standard package manager for Kubernetes. It packages Kubernetes manifests into Charts, uses templates and values to make deployments configurable, and provides built-in support for installing, upgrading, rolling back, and managing application releases. It is especially useful for deploying complex applications with multiple Kubernetes resources across different environments.
